---
// The Publishing guide: one page, laid out as the best docs are (Linear's
// start guide for its layout and rail, Tailwind's numbered steps, Vercel's
// code blocks). The article in the store's text column, the "On this page"
// rail at the container's right (below 1100 px, the same links folded under
// the lead); a quick start in three steps; what the store needs as a table,
// with its rule about Zeron's mark in full under it; the store's Markdown;
// sending, verification, versions; publishing from CI in three steps; then
// where to go next. Its facts are the engine's guide's (apps/zgx/docs/
// plugins.md, "Publishing to the store"), said for a person on the web
// (amendments 44 and 45; deploy tokens, contract a315eef5a); the reasons are
// the reviewers' own list (lib/store/admin.ts). The same for everyone, kept
// by shared caches as the store's other public pages are.
import CodeBlock from "../../components/look/CodeBlock.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/look/Icon.astro";
import Seal from "../../components/look/Seal.astro";
import Store from "../../layouts/Store.astro";
import "../../styles/look/guide.css";
import { lsb } from "../../lib/store/bearing.ts";
import { DECLINE_REASONS } from "../../lib/store/admin.ts";
import { publicPage } from "../../lib/store/http.ts";
import { CATEGORIES } from "../../lib/store/nav.ts";

export const prerender = false;
publicPage(Astro);

/** The page's parts, as the rail lists them: each part, and the parts under it. */
const TOC: { id: string; label: string; subs: { id: string; label: string }[] }[] = [
  { id: "quick-start", label: "Quick start", subs: [] },
  { id: "needs", label: "What the store needs", subs: [{ id: "optional", label: "Optional fields" }, { id: "markdown", label: "The store's Markdown" }] },
  { id: "send", label: "Sending a version", subs: [] },
  { id: "review", label: "Verification", subs: [] },
  { id: "versions", label: "Versions", subs: [] },
  { id: "ci", label: "Publishing from CI", subs: [] },
];
const label = (id: string) => TOC.flatMap((t) => [t, ...t.subs]).find((t) => t.id === id)!.label;

const OPTIONAL = `{
  "keywords": ["focus", "timer"],
  "homepage": "https://example.com/focus-timer",
  "repository": "https://github.com/you/focus-timer",
  "license": "MIT",
  "screenshots": ["shots/board.png", "shots/settings.png"],
  "engines": { "zeron": ">=0.4.0" }
}`;
const TOKENS = `zeron plugin token create --name "GitHub Actions" --plugins focus-timer,focus-board
zeron plugin token create --name "All my plugins" --all
zeron plugin token create --name "Release CI" --plugins focus-timer --expires-in-days 90
zeron plugin token list
zeron plugin token revoke <id>`;
const WORKFLOW = `- uses: actions/checkout@v4
- uses: zeronsh/zeron/actions/publish-plugin@v0.4.0
  with:
    token: \${{ secrets.ZERON_DEPLOY_TOKEN }}`;
---

<Store title="Publishing guide · Plugins · Zeron" description="How to put a plugin in Zeron's store: what it needs, how to send it, and how Zeron verifies it." canonical="/plugins/publishing" current="publish">
  <div class="wrap guide-page">
    <div class="guide-grid">
      <article class="guide">
        <header class="guide-head">
          <h1 class={lsb("Publishing guide")}>Publishing guide</h1>
          <p class="lead">Put your plugin in Zeron's store. A version is listed as soon as it passes the store's checks, and gets Zeron's check mark once a reviewer has read it.</p>
        </header>

        {/* (Narrower than the rail's room: the same links, folded under the lead.) */}
        <details class="toc-inline">
          <summary>On this page<Icon name="chev" /></summary>
          <ol>
            {TOC.map((t) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a>
                {t.subs.length > 0 && <ol>{t.subs.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}</ol>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </details>

        <section aria-labelledby="quick-start">
          <h2 id="quick-start">{label("quick-start")}<a class="anchor" href="#quick-start" aria-label={`Link to ${label("quick-start")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Check it</h3>
              <p>It lists everything the store would refuse, before anything is sent. It needs no Zeron running, so a pull request's CI can run it too.</p>
              <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="zeron plugin check <folder>" id="cb-check" />
            </li>
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Publish it</h3>
              <p>From a terminal, with Zeron running and signed in (<code>--dry-run</code> checks it without sending it). Or in Zeron: Settings › Plugins › Yours › Publish…</p>
              <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="zeron plugin publish <id>" id="cb-publish" />
            </li>
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Get verified</h3>
              <p>A reviewer reads it. Until then it's listed, saying it isn't verified yet; once they have, Zeron gives it its check mark.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="needs">
          <h2 id="needs">{label("needs")}<a class="anchor" href="#needs" aria-label={`Link to ${label("needs")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <p>Anyone can share a Zeron plugin as a file or a link. The store is for the ones you want people to find: a version that passes the store's automatic checks is listed and installable the moment you send it, and Zeron verifies it once a reviewer has read it. Zeron says which versions are verified wherever it offers one.</p>
          <p>A plugin is a folder with its <code>zeron-plugin.json</code>. For the store, it also needs:</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="gtable">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What the store needs</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>README.md</code></th><td>Its page, at most 512 KB, in the store's Markdown (below). Pictures are files in the plugin, by a relative path (<code>![Board](shots/board.png)</code>); a picture from elsewhere shows as a link, because what is shown is what was reviewed.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>description</code></th><td>One line, at most 300 characters, its summary everywhere it's listed.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>name</code></th><td>At most 64 characters.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>keywords</code></th><td>May not say Zeron's mark either: the store refuses it as <code>bad_listing</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>icon</code></th><td>An SVG in the plugin, at most 32 KB. It's drawn in the text's colour, as Zeron draws its own.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>categories</code></th><td>One or two of {CATEGORIES.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && (i === CATEGORIES.length - 1 ? " and " : ", ")}<code>{c.id}</code></>)}.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>version</code></th><td>Strict semver, <code>1.2.0</code> or <code>2.0.0-beta.1</code>. No <code>v</code>, no leading zeros.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Your username</th><td>On your Zeron account, so the store can say “by @username”. Zeron asks for one after you sign in.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Zeron's check</th><td>A name or description can't claim it: no “verified”, “official” or check mark about the plugin itself.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <details class="fold">
            <summary>Exactly what counts<Icon name="chev" /></summary>
            <p>A name may not claim Zeron's mark: the words “verified” or “official” in any spelling that reads as them (look-alike letters, letters spaced apart) or in the store's other languages (“oficial”, “officiel”, “官方”…; never an ordinary word, so “vérifie la syntaxe” and “数学公式” are fine), or a check mark. A description may use those words about other things (“opens the official Rust docs”), only not of the plugin itself (“Official Git tools”, “a verified plugin”, “is verified”), and no check mark. Neither may hold a character drawn as nothing, but an emoji's own variation selector. Its <code>keywords</code> may not say the mark either: <code>bad_listing</code>.</p>
          </details>
          <p>The first account to publish an id keeps it. An account can take up to 5 new ids in any 7 days. Zeron's own plugins' ids, ids starting with <code>zeron</code>, and names that look like a brand's are held back.</p>

          <h3 id="optional">{label("optional")}<a class="anchor" href="#optional" aria-label={`Link to ${label("optional")}`}>#</a></h3>
          <p>The rest is optional:</p>
          <CodeBlock label="zeron-plugin.json" code={OPTIONAL} id="cb-optional" />
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="gtable">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>keywords</code></th><td>Up to 8, lowercase, at most 32 characters each.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>screenshots</code></th><td>Up to 8 PNG or JPEG pictures in the plugin, shown on its page.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>engines.zeron</code></th><td>The Zeron versions it works with. Anyone on another can't install it, and its page says why.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>CHANGELOG.md</code></th><td>Each version's notes: the section under the heading that starts with that version (<code>## 1.2.0</code>). People read them when Zeron offers the update.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3 id="markdown">{label("markdown")}<a class="anchor" href="#markdown" aria-label={`Link to ${label("markdown")}`}>#</a></h3>
          <p>A README and a version's notes are drawn from this part of Markdown, on the web as in Zeron. Anything else is shown as you typed it, characters and all.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="gtable halves">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Drawn</th><th scope="col">Shown as typed</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td data-head="Drawn">
                    <ul>
                      <li>Headings (<code>#</code> to <code>######</code>, or underlined), paragraphs and line breaks.</li>
                      <li><em>Emphasis</em>, <strong>strong</strong>, <del>strikethrough</del> (<code>~~</code>), and <code>`code`</code>.</li>
                      <li>Code blocks, fenced (with a language, to highlight it) or indented.</li>
                      <li>Quotes, numbered and bulleted lists, and task lists (<code>- [ ]</code>, <code>- [x]</code>), nested up to 32 deep.</li>
                      <li>Tables, up to 64 columns.</li>
                      <li>Rules (<code>---</code>).</li>
                      <li>Links: inline, by reference, and <code>&lt;https://…&gt;</code> or a bare <code>https://</code> address. A link goes only to https or mail; a link to a file of the plugin shows as its words.</li>
                      <li>Pictures, as above, also inside a link (a badge).</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                  <td data-head="Shown as typed">
                    <ul>
                      <li>Raw HTML (<code>&lt;p align="center"&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>).</li>
                      <li>Footnotes (<code>[^1]</code>).</li>
                      <li>Math (<code>$…$</code>, a <code>$$</code> block, or a fence marked <code>math</code>): drawn in Zeron, shown as typed here.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p><code>zeron plugin check</code> says where a README or CHANGELOG has HTML, a footnote or display math, as a note, not a failure. Use Markdown for pictures and headings. Nothing in a README runs.</p>
          <p>The package, as sent, is at most 20 MB. Files that aren't text or pictures, a compiled program say, are allowed, but reviewers look closely at them.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="send">
          <h2 id="send">{label("send")}<a class="anchor" href="#send" aria-label={`Link to ${label("send")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <p>Check it first: <code>zeron plugin check &lt;folder&gt;</code> lists everything the store would refuse, before anything is sent. Then send it, from Zeron or a terminal:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>In Zeron: Settings › Plugins › Yours › Publish…</li>
            <li>From a terminal, with Zeron running and signed in: <code>zeron plugin publish &lt;id&gt;</code>. <code>--dry-run</code> checks it without sending it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The plugin is signed with your device's key and sent with your account. <a href="/account">Your plugins</a> shows each version's state, and a reviewer's words when there are some.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="review">
          <h2 id="review">{label("review")}<a class="anchor" href="#review" aria-label={`Link to ${label("review")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <ul class="flow" aria-label="A version's states">
            <li><span class="state quiet">Listed</span><Icon name="arrow" /><span class="state ok"><Seal size="s" quiet />Verified</span></li>
            <li><span class="state quiet">Listed</span><Icon name="arrow" /><span class="state bad">Not verified</span><span class="flow-why">with the reviewer's reason</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>Every version is listed once it passes the automatic checks: the package, its signature, its size, its version, new programs and hosts. A reviewer then reads it. They look at what it asks for (what's new since your last verified version first, and anything that reaches past Zeron most closely), what changed in its files, and its page as visitors will see it.</p>
          <p>Zeron verifies it, or doesn't, with one of these reasons and the reviewer's note:</p>
          <ul class="reasons">
            {DECLINE_REASONS.map((r) => <li>{r.label}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>A version that isn't verified stays listed: everyone sees only that it isn't verified yet, and installing it always asks first. You read the reason and the reviewer's note in Zeron, the command line and <a href="/account">Your plugins</a>; send the fix as a new version. Say plainly in the README what your plugin runs and where it connects; it's what a reviewer asks for most.</p>
          <p>Anyone signed in can report a listed plugin. The reviewers can take a harmful version down, which turns it off on the devices that have it, or remove one that shouldn't be in the store; its author reads why.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="versions">
          <h2 id="versions">{label("versions")}<a class="anchor" href="#versions" aria-label={`Link to ${label("versions")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Each version is sent once and keeps its content for good. Each must be later than every version you sent before.</li>
            <li>Every verified version can be installed, and so can the newest; each says whether it's verified.</li>
            <li>A version that isn't verified stops taking new installs once a newer one is published: a release replaces older releases and pre-releases, a pre-release older pre-releases only. It stays so unless a reviewer verifies it later. Copies already installed keep working.</li>
            <li>A pre-release (<code>2.0.0-beta.1</code>) is offered only to people who chose that plugin's pre-releases.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop new installs</strong> of a version (those who have it keep it working), and allow them again, except for a version a newer one replaced. From Your plugins, Zeron, or <code>zeron plugin yank|unyank &lt;id&gt; &lt;version&gt;</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="ci">
          <h2 id="ci">{label("ci")}<a class="anchor" href="#ci" aria-label={`Link to ${label("ci")}`}>#</a></h2>
          <p>A <strong>deploy token</strong> lets a CI job, such as a GitHub Action, send new versions without your session. A token is your account's, with a scope you choose when you make it and can't change: the plugins it names, or all of yours, those you publish later included. A plugin's first version always comes from your machine; a token sends the ones after it.</p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Make a token</h3>
              <p>On <a href="/account/tokens">Deploy tokens</a>, in Zeron (Settings, Plugins, Deploy tokens), or from a terminal with Zeron running and signed in:</p>
              <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code={TOKENS} id="cb-tokens" />
            </li>
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Add it to your CI's secrets</h3>
              <p>Put the token in your CI's secrets as <code>ZERON_DEPLOY_TOKEN</code>, then publish with it. It's read from the environment only, never from an argument, and needs no Zeron running and nobody signed in:</p>
              <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="ZERON_DEPLOY_TOKEN=zdt_… zeron plugin publish <folder>" id="cb-env" />
            </li>
            <li class="step">
              <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
              <h3 class="step-title">Publish</h3>
              <p>On GitHub, the publish action installs Zeron's command line and sends the plugin with your token:</p>
              <CodeBlock label=".github/workflows/publish.yml" code={WORKFLOW} id="cb-workflow" />
              <p>Pin it to a Zeron release tag or a commit, never a branch: the step holds your token. Without a token (a pull request from a fork gets no secrets) it only checks.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <ul>
            <li>Making one asks you to sign in again first, wherever you make it. <code>--name</code> says what it's for. Its scope is <code>--plugins a,b</code> (plugins you already have in the store, up to 50) or <code>--all</code> (every plugin of yours, those you first publish later included, never one transferred to you), fixed once made: another set of plugins is another token. <code>--expires-in-days</code> stops it working after that many days.</li>
            <li>The token (<code>zdt_…</code>) is shown once, as it's made. Neither Zeron nor the store keeps it, so one that's lost can't be shown again: revoke it and make another.</li>
            <li><code>token list</code> shows your tokens: each one's id, its short id (the first characters of the token), name and what it publishes; never the tokens themselves. So does <a href="/account/tokens">Deploy tokens</a>, with when each was last used and when it ends.</li>
            <li><code>token revoke</code> takes the id <code>token list</code> shows. It publishes nothing from then on; what it published stays.</li>
            <li>An account has at most 20 live tokens, and makes at most 10 a day.</li>
            <li>It exits 0 when published, 1 when the plugin doesn't meet the store's rules, 2 for a set-up problem (a mistyped token among them: it's checked before anything is sent), 3 when the store refused the token or the account, and 4 when the store couldn't be reached.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>A token sends new versions of the plugins in its scope, nothing else, and each is listed as any version is, not yet verified; your devices tell you when a token publishes one, by its name. A version it sends is installable at once, so treat a token like a password: if one leaks, revoke it (from <a href="/account/tokens">Deploy tokens</a>, Zeron, or <code>zeron plugin token revoke</code>) and stop new installs of what it sent.</p>
        </section>

        <nav class="next" aria-label="Where to go next">
          <a class="ncard" href="/account"><span class="nc-t">Your plugins<Icon name="arrow" /></span><span class="nc-l">Each version's state, and a reviewer's words when there are some.</span></a>
          <a class="ncard" href="/account/tokens"><span class="nc-t">Deploy tokens<Icon name="arrow" /></span><span class="nc-l">Make, see and revoke the tokens your CI publishes with.</span></a>
        </nav>
      </article>

      <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="toc-h">On this page</p>
        <ol data-toc>
          {TOC.map((t) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a>
              {t.subs.length > 0 && <ol>{t.subs.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}</ol>}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Store>
